# Repository-wide line-ending rule (FP-156).
#
# core.autocrlf is true on this project's Windows development machines, so
# before this file existed every text file outside services/host/ - which has
# had its own rule since T-001 - was written into the working tree with CRLF,
# while `svc:make` writes generated files directly with LF. A byte-for-byte
# regeneration diff then failed for someone who had changed nothing, and Pint's
# line_ending fixer rejected the checked-out PHP files outright, so `composer
# check` died at its first step on a fresh clone.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text; `eol=lf` makes every text file land
# in the working tree with LF on every platform, whatever core.autocrlf says.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The two Windows-only script formats keep CRLF, so this rule agrees with the
# root .editorconfig's `[*.{bat,ps1}] end_of_line = crlf` rather than fighting
# it - and with the .NET print agent's tooling when it lands.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary, so git never inspects or translates anything inside it.
*.ico binary
